                <h2>The Emergency Meeting at 2 AM</h2>
<p>"GMV dropped 18% week-over-week, while ad spend increased by 23%."</p>
<p>This was the weekly report received by Xiao Lin, operations lead at a tool e-commerce independent site, three days before peak season launch. The team held an emergency meeting—operations, marketing, and finance stared at the dashboard data in silence for ten minutes.</p>
<p>The problem was clear: money was being spent, but conversions were declining. But what was the cause? Where should the budget go?</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Among the independent site clients we've worked with in the past six months, over 60% face similar "budget misfocus" issues before peak seasons—accounts have sufficient balance, but delivery becomes chaotic, ultimately driving up CPA and eroding margins.</p>
<p>Here's the complete breakdown of this case.</p>
<h2>Problem Identification: Three Signals of Budget Imbalance</h2>
<p>After reviewing the data, Xiao Lin's team found three clear anomalies:</p>
<p><strong>Signal 1: Rigid Meta vs. Google Budget Ratio</strong></p>
<p>The independent site's monthly ad budget averaged $80,000, with Meta at 65%, Google at 30%, and TikTok at only 5%. This ratio was set six months ago and had barely been adjusted since. However, recent backend data showed TikTok's CPA was already 40% lower than Meta's, while ROAS was 15% higher. The budget structure clearly lagged behind channel performance.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 2: Best-seller Devouring 80% of Budget</strong></p>
<p>The site had a $49 tool kit as a consistent bestseller, and the marketing team habitually allocated 80% of budget to this product. But during peak season, new customer acquisition cost (Landing Cost) increased 2.3x, while repeat purchase rate declined—indicating traffic was repeatedly targeting the same user base, hitting a growth ceiling.</p>
<p><strong>Signal 3: Misaligned Recharge Timing and Billing Cycles</strong></p>
<p>The team recharged on the 5th of each month, but Google's and Meta's billing cycles differed, sometimes causing account balance shortages that forced bid reductions, and sometimes leaving funds idle. Finance reconciliation showed that missed conversion opportunities due to recharge timing issues accounted for approximately 12% over the past three months.</p>
<h2>Diagnosis: It's Not About Insufficient Funds, But Improper Placement</h2>
<p>After confirming the problems, two opinions emerged internally: one suggested directly cutting underperforming channels, the other suggested increasing spend to "win through volume."</p>
<p>Xiao Lin's judgment was: <strong>This isn't a total budget problem, but a distribution structure problem.</strong></p>
<p>He required the marketing team to complete three tasks within 24 hours:</p>
<ol>
<li>Export 90-day CPA, ROAS, conversion volume, and traffic structure data for all channels</li>
<li>Recalculate comprehensive channel performance using "Traffic Efficiency = Conversions × AOV ÷ Ad Spend"</li>
<li>Simulate peak season traffic demand gaps and determine "primary channel" vs. "testing channel" budget ratios</li>
</ol>
<p>After the data analysis, results were surprising: what was originally considered "money-burning" TikTok was actually the highest comprehensive traffic efficiency channel, while Google Shopping—highly anticipated—performed poorly for product categories under $50 AOV.</p>
<h2>Actions Taken: Three-Step Adjustment in 72 Hours</h2>
<p><strong>Step 1: Redefine Budget Ratios (6 hours)</strong></p>
<p>Based on data modeling, the team adjusted budget ratios to: Meta 45%, Google 20%, TikTok 25%, contingency testing fund 10%. Daily budget caps were also set to prevent single-channel overspending.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Tiered Product Budget Allocation (12 hours)</strong></p>
<p>Products were divided into three categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Traffic Products</strong> (bestseller tool kit): Maintain spend but cap budget share at 40%, targeting new customers</li>
<li><strong>Profit Products</strong> (accessories, peripherals): Increase bids and budget allocation, targeting higher AOV</li>
<li><strong>Testing Products</strong> (new arrivals): Use contingency funds for small-scale A/B tests, scale only after validation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 3: Align Recharge Timing with Billing Cycles (8 hours)</strong></p>
<p>Finance restructured the Google and Meta billing calendars, splitting monthly recharges into two installments: 50% on the 1st and 50% on the 15th. Account balance alerts were set to trigger automatically when balance fell below 20%.</p>
<h2>Results Tracking: Data Changes After Two Weeks</h2>
<p>Following adjustments, the team tracked data continuously for two weeks:</p>
<ul>
<li>Overall CPA dropped 31%, from $28 to $19.3</li>
<li>TikTok channel ROAS improved from 2.1 to 3.4</li>
<li>Traffic product share decreased from 80% to 42%, while profit product revenue increased 27%</li>
<li>Account balance volatility narrowed from ±35% to ±12%</li>
</ul>
<p>More importantly, the team established a monthly budget review mechanism: channel efficiency reports on the 25th of each month, with quarterly budget ratio adjustments.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't a one-time decision set in stone—it requires continuous dynamic adjustment based on channel performance, product mix, and market competition.</p>
<p>If you're also feeling "don't know where to spend money" before peak season, stop and ask yourself three questions:</p>
<ol>
<li>Is my channel traffic efficiency data based on the last 30 days of actual conversions?</li>
<li>When was the last time my budget ratios were adjusted?</li>
<li>Does my account recharge timing match my delivery plan?</li>
</ol>
<p>If the answers are "it's been a while" or "based on gut feeling," you may need a systematic budget health diagnosis.</p>
